	'stories_h1' => 'Graduate Stories',
	'stories_lead' => 'Our graduates work as professional interpreters across Esterhaven’s healthcare services, judicial courts, and educational classrooms. Here, four former students describe their backgrounds, their hardest Saturday simulations, and their working lives today.',
	'stories_s1_h' => 'Lukas Petrov: healthcare interpreter at Esterhaven General Hospital',
	'stories_s1_p' => 'Lukas Petrov graduated with the Interpreter Diploma in 2021 and now works as a staff healthcare interpreter in the outpatient department at Esterhaven General Hospital. Before enrolling at Yubi College, Lukas worked as a hospital porter, pushing stretchers through the same corridors he now walks as an interpreter. He recalls his hardest Saturday simulation vividly: interpreting a complex oncology consultation with role-player Pieter playing a Deaf patient receiving unexpected lab results. Lukas froze momentarily on medication instructions, but the afternoon table debrief helped him dissect his hesitation calmly. Today, Lukas interprets daily clinical appointments, diagnostic imaging procedures, and specialist consultations across outpatient clinics, providing clear, compassionate communication where he once transported medical equipment.',
	'stories_s1_sub' => '01 · Lukas',
	'stories_s2_h' => 'Mireille Santos: court interpreter in magistrates’ and family courts',
	'stories_s2_p' => 'Mireille Santos completed the Interpreter Diploma in 2019 and followed it with the Specialist Certificate in Legal Settings in 2020. A former commercial paralegal, Mireille sought a career with direct human impact. Her most challenging Saturday in training involved a rapid-fire criminal bail hearing simulation run by Elias Brandt, where conflicting witness testimonies and formal statutory phrasing pushed her cognitive processing to its limit. At the table debrief, Elias showed her how to manage processing lag without sacrificing syntactic accuracy. Mireille now interprets regularly at Esterhaven Magistrates’ Court and Quayside Family Court, handling sensitive custody hearings, civil disputes, and criminal arraignments with the rigorous neutrality and procedural precision instilled during her studies.',
	'stories_s2_sub' => '02 · Mireille',
	'stories_s3_h' => 'Hana Okafor: educational interpreter at Linden Bank School',
	'stories_s3_p' => 'Hana Okafor earned her Interpreter Diploma in 2022 and currently serves as an educational interpreter in the deaf unit at Linden Bank School. Having worked for years as a classroom teaching assistant, Hana understood school dynamics but wanted professional qualifications to interpret academic content. Her most demanding Saturday simulation involved a tripartite meeting with an anxious role-player parent, an impatient teacher, and a Deaf pupil, requiring swift register adjustments between adult conversation and child-directed signing. The afternoon debrief taught her how to maintain professional boundaries while keeping the student fully included. Today, Hana interprets mathematics lessons, science laboratories, and school assemblies, ensuring Deaf children have equal access to every aspect of their education.',
	'stories_s3_sub' => '03 · Hana',
	'stories_s4_h' => 'Daniel Reyes: community interpreter in healthcare and schools',
	'stories_s4_p' => 'Daniel Reyes graduated with the Interpreter Diploma in 2023. Growing up with Deaf parents, Daniel had signed fluently since childhood, but he enrolled at Yubi College to replace informal habit with professional discipline. His hardest Saturday came during a simulated pediatric emergency, where his natural instinct to advocate for the family clashed with the strict impartiality required of a professional interpreter. During the table debrief, Helena Varga helped him understand how disciplined neutrality protects both the Deaf client and the practitioner. Daniel now works across Esterhaven as an independent community interpreter, facilitating communication at neighborhood GP surgeries, clinical clinics, and school parents’ evenings with certified professional standard.',
	'stories_s4_sub' => '04 · Daniel',
	'stories_sub' => 'Four graduates',
	'story_by' => 'Noor Haddad, Deaf language tutor',
	'story_h' => 'Saturday, 14:10',
	'story_link' => 'How a teaching week runs →',
	'story_p1' => 'In the morning role-play, Tomasz stood in the mock outpatient room interpreting between a doctor and a Deaf patient played by Pieter. The doctor explained an unfamiliar treatment schedule, and Tomasz, trying to soften bad news, altered the dosage phrasing. Pieter stopped the exercise at once: softening a diagnosis takes the choice away from the patient.',
	'story_p2' => 'At two o’clock the group gathered around the long table with notebooks closed. Noor asked Tomasz to replay the explanation in sign, exactly as he had given it. From the observer’s chair, Adaeze pointed out where his face had hesitated and shown his doubt to the patient. They took the sentence apart handshape by handshape until the medical meaning was direct and clear.',
	'story_p3' => 'This is why the college works this way. You keep your job, studying two evenings and on Saturdays. You learn language from Deaf tutors in groups of nine, interpret in pairs in the lab, and work through hospital, court and school scenes with role-players. Everything that happens in the morning is talked through at the table the same afternoon.',
	'story_q' => 'Don’t tell me what the doctor said. Show me what the patient understood. That is the job, and it is the part nobody can do for you.',
	'story_sub' => '02 · A story from the table',
	'strip_label' => 'A teaching week, five stops',
	'taster_email_button' => 'Book a place by email',
	'taster_evenings_h1' => 'Taster Evenings',
	'taster_evenings_lead' => 'Our free taster evenings give prospective students a hands-on introduction to Yubi College, our Deaf tutors, and the physical space at 3 Weaver’s Yard. No prior knowledge of sign language is necessary to attend.',
	'taster_evenings_s1_h' => 'Upcoming dates and the evening schedule',
	'taster_evenings_s1_p' => 'Taster evenings run from 18:30 to 20:00 on the first Tuesday of the month: Tuesday 2 February, 2 March, and 6 April 2027. The evening is structured to give you a genuine feel for how we teach. At 18:30, admissions officer Yara Lindqvist welcomes guests with tea and an overview of the college. At 18:45, you take a seat in a circle with Deaf tutor Noor Haddad or Pieter Laine to learn your first twenty signs in an engaging, voice-off session. At 19:30, we discuss course structures, fees, and monthly instalment options, concluding promptly at 20:00.',
	'taster_evenings_s1_sub' => '01 · Dates and the evening',
	'taster_evenings_s2_h' => 'Building accessibility and travel directions',
	'taster_evenings_s2_p' => 'Yubi College is located at 3 Weaver’s Yard, Calloway Street, Esterhaven, a peaceful cobbled courtyard situated just five minutes on foot from the Esterhaven Quay tram stop, served by tram lines 2 and 5. Bicycle racks are available in the courtyard. The college building has a completely step-free entrance from street level, and an induction hearing loop is operational in reception and teaching rooms. Qualified spoken language and sign language interpreters are present throughout every taster evening to ensure complete accessibility for Deaf and hearing attendees alike.',
	'taster_evenings_s2_sub' => '02 · Access',
	'taster_evenings_s3_h' => 'How do you book a place at a taster evening?',
	'taster_evenings_s3_p' => 'Attendance at all taster evenings is completely free, but advance reservation is required because our teaching rooms accommodate only small groups. You can book your place online by submitting your details, or by emailing hello@yubicollege.example with the subject line booking request. If you prefer to speak with our admissions officer, you can call our voice number at +00 41 555 0187, or send a text or video call to +00 41 555 0188. Yara Lindqvist will confirm your reservation and assist with any specific access requirements you might need.',
	'taster_evenings_s3_sub' => '03 · Book a place',
	'taster_evenings_sub' => 'Free, no signing needed',
	'taster_mail_subject' => 'Taster evening booking',
	'td1_d' => 'Tuesday 2 February 2027',
	'td1_t' => '18:30–20:00',
	'td2_d' => 'Tuesday 2 March 2027',
	'td2_t' => '18:30–20:00',
	'td3_d' => 'Tuesday 6 April 2027',
	'td3_t' => '18:30–20:00',
	'teaching_week_h1' => 'A Teaching Week',
	'teaching_week_lead' => 'Second-year Interpreter Diploma students balance ten hours of college teaching and two hours of home study alongside their daytime jobs. Every week follows a deliberate progression from language refinement to lab practice, culminating in Saturday setting simulations.',
	'teaching_week_s1_h' => 'Tuesday evening: language with a Deaf tutor',
	'teaching_week_s1_p' => 'On Tuesday from 18:30 to 21:00, nine students sit in a circle of chairs with their Deaf tutor. For the first hour, spoken voices are entirely prohibited. The room is silent except for the movement of hands, visual focus, and facial expressions. Students work on expanding spatial syntax, mastering regional variations, and sharpening vocabulary precision. Because the group is limited to nine, every learner participates continuously under the direct guidance of tutors like Noor Haddad or Pieter Laine, ensuring that linguistic habits are refined before interpreting exercises begin later in the week.',
	'teaching_week_s1_sub' => '01 · Tuesday',
	'teaching_week_s2_h' => 'Wednesday: two hours of recorded practice at home',
	'teaching_week_s2_p' => 'Wednesday evenings require no travel to Calloway Street. Instead, students dedicate two hours at home to focused independent study using recorded source material. Each student works through three recorded spoken and signed dialogues, practicing simultaneous and consecutive voicing and signing. Learners record their responses, identify dropped concepts or hesitated vocabulary, and log their reflections directly into their physical practice books. This solitary drill builds the cognitive stamina and mental processing speed required before stepping into the booth room on Thursday evening, turning language knowledge into automatic reflex.',
	'teaching_week_s2_sub' => '02 · Wednesday',
	'teaching_week_s3_h' => 'Thursday evening: the interpreting lab',
	'teaching_week_s3_p' => 'Thursday from 18:30 to 21:00 brings students into the interpreting lab at 3 Weaver’s Yard. Working in pairs inside the six glass-fronted booths, one student puts on the headset to interpret a live or recorded scenario while their partner sits beside them, actively noting hesitations, omissions, and clarity. Halfway through the session, the partners swap roles. Head of interpreting practice Elias Brandt circulates between booths, observing technique through the glass and offering immediate corrections. This peer-supported practice removes performance anxiety while instilling rigorous habits of mutual critique and precision.',
	'teaching_week_s3_sub' => '03 · Thursday',
	'teaching_week_s4_h' => 'Saturday morning: setting practice with role-players',
	'teaching_week_s4_p' => 'Saturday mornings from 09:30 to 12:30 move students from the controlled lab into realistic community environments. Professional role-players and Deaf actors enact genuine scenarios across mock hospital consulting rooms, magistrate benches, and school offices. A different setting is tackled each week, confronting students with difficult medical diagnoses, tense courtroom examinations, or emotionally charged parent meetings. Students interpret in pairs, experiencing the physical and cognitive pressures of real-world assignments under the direct observation of experienced faculty. Making mistakes in these safe, simulated rooms prevents errors from ever occurring in actual public service.',
	'teaching_week_s4_sub' => '04 · Saturday',
	'teaching_week_s5_h' => 'Saturday afternoon: the table debrief',
	'teaching_week_s5_p' => 'From 14:00 to 15:00 on Saturday, the entire cohort gathers around the long wooden table with their tutors. All notebooks, clipboards, and pens are set aside. For one hour, the group talks through the morning’s scenarios openly and without formal grading. Students discuss where they felt uncertain, which linguistic choices conveyed accurate meaning, and how professional boundaries were maintained. Tutors share insights from their own decades in public service, demystifying the emotional weight of interpreting. By 15:00, the teaching week is complete, allowing students to return to their jobs refreshed.',
	'teaching_week_s5_sub' => '05 · Saturday',
	'teaching_week_s6_h' => 'What does the full academic year look like?',
	'teaching_week_s6_p' => 'The academic year runs from September to June, encompassing 34 Saturdays structured into three immersive setting blocks. From September to December, training focuses exclusively on healthcare settings, including outpatient clinics, inpatient wards, and emergency departments. From January to March, the curriculum transitions into legal settings, covering police stations, magistrates’ courts, and family tribunals. From April to June, the focus shifts to educational settings, addressing classroom support, specialist examinations, and multidisciplinary meetings. This structured rotation ensures every student gains broad familiarity across Esterhaven’s major public institutions before entering their final placement hours.',
	'teaching_week_s6_sub' => '06 · The year',
	'teaching_week_sub' => 'Ten hours a week, two at home',
	'tst_c1' => 'Write to Yara',
	'tst_c2' => 'Term letters',
	'tst_cta' => 'Book a place',
	'tst_facts' => 'Tuesday 2 February, 2 March and 6 April 2027 · 18:30–20:00 · 3 Weaver’s Yard',
	'tst_h' => 'Come to a taster evening',
	'tst_p' => 'Spend an hour and a half with Noor or Pieter learning your first twenty signs. Meet Yara and ask about courses, timetables and fees. The room at 3 Weaver’s Yard is step-free and has a hearing loop.',
	'tst_sub' => '07 · Free, no signing needed',
	'tut_c1_p' => 'Deaf language tutor. Leads the Tuesday circle and every first signs evening.',
	'tut_c1_t' => 'Noor Haddad',
	'tut_c2_p' => 'Head of interpreting practice. Runs the Thursday lab and the legal certificate.',
	'tut_c2_t' => 'Elias Brandt',
	'tutors_h1' => 'Tutors',
	'tutors_lead' => 'Our faculty combines experienced Deaf educators and veteran public service interpreters who work actively in hospitals, courtrooms, and schools. We teach in small groups where every student receives continuous individual guidance and feedback.',
	'tutors_s1_h' => 'Why is our language teaching Deaf-led?',
	'tutors_s1_p' => 'At Yubi College, every sign language class is taught by a Deaf tutor. We believe that true linguistic competence cannot be separated from the lived culture, history, and community of Deaf people. In our classrooms, spoken voice is set aside, requiring students to communicate through visual attention, physical gesture, and spatial awareness. This immersive approach builds natural fluency, instills deep respect for Deaf autonomy, and ensures that interpreters graduate with the cultural sensitivity required for professional public service.',
	'tutors_s1_sub' => '01 · Deaf-led',
	'tutors_s2_h' => 'Language and Deaf studies: Noor Haddad and Pieter Laine',
	'tutors_s2_p' => 'Noor Haddad is our lead Deaf language tutor. A Deaf educator in her 40s with extensive teaching experience, she guides both Foundation and Diploma students through core sign language grammar and conversational fluency, bringing patience, precision, and warmth to every evening circle. Pieter Laine is our lecturer in Deaf studies. A Deaf scholar in his 60s with a distinctive white beard, Pieter lectures on Deaf culture, linguistic history, and community language structures. Together, Noor and Pieter ensure that students master sign language as a living, expressive language rather than a mechanical code.',
	'tutors_s2_sub' => '02 · Language',
	'tutors_s3_h' => 'Interpreting practice: Helena Varga and Elias Brandt',
	'tutors_s3_p' => 'Helena Varga is the principal of Yubi College. A hospital and conference interpreter for 22 years, Helena has led the college since 2015, maintaining our rigorous vocational standards and public service ethos. Elias Brandt serves as head of interpreting practice, managing the Thursday interpreting lab and directing the Specialist Certificate in Legal Settings. An active court interpreter with decades of courtroom experience, Elias mentors students through the technical mechanics of booth work, simultaneous voicing, and the demanding ethical responsibilities of legal interpreting.',
	'tutors_s3_sub' => '03 · Interpreting',
	'tutors_s4_h' => 'Healthcare and placements: Ruth Abiodun and Clara Moreau',
	'tutors_s4_p' => 'Ruth Abiodun leads our healthcare interpreting training and directs the Specialist Certificate in Healthcare. A healthcare interpreter in her 50s, Ruth brings decades of acute clinical experience into our Saturday setting simulations, preparing students for the emotional and communicative complexities of medical consultations. Clara Moreau is our placements coordinator, managing relationships across Esterhaven’s 11 partner hospitals, courts, and schools. Clara coordinates all 60 hours of second-year student shadowing, conducting site visits to observe students and ensure that every placement meets our strict supervisory standards.',
	'tutors_s4_sub' => '04 · Placements',
	'tutors_s5_h' => 'Admissions and student support: Yara Lindqvist',
	'tutors_s5_p' => 'Yara Lindqvist is our admissions officer and the first point of contact for anyone interested in studying at Yubi College. Yara personally answers every enquiry submitted by email, voice telephone, text, or video call. She guides prospective students through course selection, coordinates the monthly taster evenings, manages application paperwork, and schedules entry assessments. Whether you are wondering about entry requirements, monthly fee instalments, or building accessibility at 3 Weaver’s Yard, Yara provides friendly, detailed, and prompt advice to every applicant.',
	'tutors_s5_sub' => '05 · Admissions',
	'tutors_sub' => 'Seven people, named',
	'wk1_d' => 'TUESDAY · 18:30–21:00',
	'wk1_h' => 'Language with a Deaf tutor',
	'wk1_p' => 'Nine people sit in a circle of chairs with no voices for the first hour, building natural signing fluency and speed.',
	'wk2_d' => 'WEDNESDAY · AT HOME',
	'wk2_h' => 'Two hours at home',
	'wk2_p' => 'Three recorded dialogues to voice and sign back on your own, with every question and reflection logged in your practice book.',
	'wk3_d' => 'THURSDAY · 18:30–21:00',
	'wk3_h' => 'The interpreting lab',
	'wk3_p' => 'In pairs in the booth room, one student interprets incoming dialogue while the other watches and notes, then you swap.',
	'wk4_d' => 'SATURDAY · 09:30–12:30',
	'wk4_h' => 'A setting morning',
	'wk4_p' => 'A hospital, court or school scene played by role-players, so you meet a different public setting every week.',
	'wk5_d' => 'SATURDAY · 14:00–15:00',
	'wk5_h' => 'The table',
	'wk5_p' => 'The whole group talks the morning through with the tutors, notebooks closed, every decision discussed.',
	'wk_court_h' => 'In a court',
	'wk_court_p' => 'Observe preliminary hearings, magistrates’ trials and family tribunals, and learn the oath and the procedure.',
	'wk_court_s' => 'ESTERHAVEN MAGISTRATES\' · QUAYSIDE FAMILY',
	'wk_h' => 'A teaching week',
	'wk_hosp_h' => 'In a hospital',
	'wk_hosp_p' => 'Shadow consultations, outpatient appointments and clinical procedures with a working interpreter.',
	'wk_hosp_s' => 'ESTERHAVEN GENERAL · NORTHGATE CHILDREN\'S',
	'wk_line' => 'Ten hours of teaching, two at home, and nothing on Monday or Friday. This is the week Tomasz and Adaeze have, from September to June, with a different setting every Saturday.',
	'wk_link' => 'The whole week, hour by hour →',
	'wk_school_h' => 'In a school',
	'wk_school_p' => 'Support lessons, assemblies and parents’ evenings in a deaf unit and in ordinary primary classrooms.',
	'wk_school_s' => 'LINDEN BANK · HARROW FIELD',
